fix(nn): the lowrank memory estimate is a floor, and 4.7% is still unexplained

Job 1677750 PASSED all five cells on L40S sm_89, but one passing number should
not have passed. The lowrank cell predicted 0.281 GiB against 0.313 GiB measured
-- an 11.4% shortfall that slipped under the harness's 15% tolerance.

Cause: gate_activation_bytes had no gate_rank parameter, so it applied the
depthwise formula to a lowrank gate and omitted the shared bottleneck activation
entirely. Now threaded through from KimiDeltaAttentionConfig.

THE FIX DOES NOT CLOSE THE GAP, AND THE COMMENT SAYS SO. The bottleneck term is
3/16 of one stream tensor against six of them, so 3.1% -- it explains 6.5 of the
11.4 points and leaves 4.7 unexplained, most likely the two up-projections' own
saved inputs. So:

  - the docstring calls the lowrank figure a FLOOR, not a prediction
  - the test asserts the bottleneck's true size (3.125%) rather than a range wide
    enough to look like it explains everything
  - the harness tolerance is per structure: 5% for depthwise, 10% for lowrank

I first wrote a single 5% tolerance and a test asserting the bottleneck closed
5-15% of the gap. That test failed, which is the only reason the residual got
found instead of buried. Tuning one threshold until the arithmetic looked right
would have shipped a number that reads as validated and is not.

The depthwise figure IS a prediction: 2.250 GiB against 2.250 GiB measured, so
5% there is comfortable rather than arbitrary.

Also adds M18, and fixes M8 -- whose pattern went stale when the return became
'total = ...'. The NO-OP detector caught it, which is the second time in two
commits that it has stopped a fictional verdict.

67 CPU tests, 7 fla-gated. 20 mutations, 0 survived, 0 no-op.

